{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791296777,"nonce":"bzRjlrlGpMjrL4jC315Tixx8A98=","title":"Link only the private library string literals that reached bodies use","message":"Lowering copies all prelowered globals into every program. For Lua any_field the shared library alone contributes 2693 globals; keeping a private string literal only when a reached body references it leaves 307 total globals. Keep named declarations, host-visible globals, runtime state, all globals for entryless/open library compilation, and the existing conservative path for deferred undecoded bodies. Global references must remain valid through split snapshot relocation and later module linking.\n\nNUC five alternating checked rounds over all Lua kernels save roughly 1-3 ms wall in many rows: any_field 26.862 -\u003e 23.888 ms, fib 39.475 -\u003e 37.474, free_function_call 42.454 -\u003e 38.718, branchy 98.923 -\u003e 96.480. Python guards similarly save 1-2 ms in many rows. Repeats resolve method_call/op_overload_ref noise, but Lua tokenize remains a regression (nine alternating rounds: kernel 171.199 -\u003e 178.638 ms; wall 213.796 -\u003e 218.532). Investigate compilation scheduling before landing this as a blanket speed improvement.\n\nFive prelowered-linking tests and Lua 43/Python 50 LLVM conformance categories pass locally; all 22 supported Python speed-kernel checks pass on the NUC.","files":null}]}